  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 218 PM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nCURRENTS & TONIGHT: Taking a look across the area, a dreary start to \nNovember continues as areas of low stratus and mid-level clouds sit \nover the area. While some thinning and southeast surface winds may \nhelp temperatures sneak into the upper 50s to mid 60s this \nafternoon, should see holes fill back in by this evening as warm air \nadvection (WAA) strengthens. Similar to last night, decided the \ndynamics at play will likely slow cooling overnight so used a blend \nof the CONSshort and NBM to slightly increase temperatures to the \nmid to upper 50s. From here, the focus will then shifts towards the \nincreasing rain chances (70%-100%) this evening and overnight. \nLooking aloft, a strengthening nocturnal LLJ along with \nincreasing lift ahead of the approaching wave will likely spur \nbands of light showers starting this evening. Lastly, the SPG \nwill continue to tighten heading into Monday morning leading to \ngusts between 30-40 mph throughout the day. With this the stage \nis set for Monday's Colorado Low.\n\nMONDAY-WEDNESDAY: Looking into the new week, we're still on track \nfor widespread rain on Monday as the developing Colorado Low lifts \ninto central and eastern SoDAK from the Nebraska Panhandle. From \nhere, not much has changed on the forecast front. Bands of light to \nmoderate showers will develop during the morning hours giving us \nmost of our accumulations. Still can't rule out a few isolated \nthunderstorms mainly across areas along and east of Highway-81 as \nabout 300-500 J/kg of instability develops. A dry slot will \ngradually progress across the area from late morning to early \nevening potentially switching us over to drizzle or just mostly dry \nconditions temporarily. Next round of precipitation comes by Tuesday \nmorning as the 850mb cold front and surface low progress through the \narea. Depending on how and if things develop around the surface low \nduring this time, we might have to be on the look out for a few \nfunnels given the enhanced stretching potential with the low and \nlower LCLs. This is particularly evident in the NAM and RAP13 NST \nwhich has scattered values in the 0.5 to 1.5 range. \n\nA tight SPG will continue to promote breezy to strong surface winds \non Tuesday with northwesterly winds behind the front producing gusts \nbetween 30-40 mph with isolated higher across southcentral SD. \nOtherwise, should see light showers hang around for most of Tuesday \nand into potentially Wednesday as the mid-level low continues to \ngradually rotate eastwards into Minnesota. Shifting gears to overall \namounts, some lingering uncertainty remain on where the heaviest \naccumulations will occur. However, guidance has shown consistent \nenough signals for a widespread 0.75\" to 1.25\" inches through at \nleast Tuesday. Nonetheless, there still could be an area of slightly \nhigher amounts (1\"-2\"+) potentially west of a Gregory to Mitchell to \nDe Smet line depending on how the low tracks through the region. \nImpact-wise, lower precipitation rates will keep the flooding \nconcerns on the lower side through the period though localized \nponding will be possible in the area of heaviest accumulation and \nlow-lying spots. Lastly, the increased precipitation  chances \nwill trend conditions cooler through Wednesday. As a result, \nhighs are expected to tumble from the 60s to low 70s on Monday \nto mid 40s to low 50s by Wednesday. \n\nTHURSDAY ONWARDS: Heading into the later parts of the week, quieter \nconditions return by Thursday as mid-level ridging progresses \nthrough the region. Next potential rain chances return by Saturday \nas a quick and compact wave dives across the northern plains. \nOtherwise, daily temperatures will continue to hover in a more \nseasonable range with highs mainly in the low to mid 50s and \novernight lows in the upper 30s to low 40s.",
